Q: Is this async-await scenario just a twist on normal function recursion or is something else taking palce? A: <p>You call <code>EventHandler</code> globally, which calls <code>EventCtrlRouter</code> that calls <code>Updater</code> that calls <code>EventCtrlRouter</code>. It's <a href="https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/mutual-recursion-example-hofstadter-female-male-sequences/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">mutual recursion</a>, where your worry is that things could go wrong, but that only happens when there is a bug, a logical mistake in the end signs or so. However, here let's see <code>EventCtrlsRouter</code> more closely:</p>
<pre><code>async function EventCtrlsRouter (caller, data) {
  console.log(`EventCtrlsRouter invoked by caller ${caller}`);
  switch (caller) {
    case &quot;EventHandler&quot;: {
       console.log(&quot;EventCtrlsRouter awaiting a call to Updater.&quot;);
       let rsp = await Updater({&quot;data&quot;:data});
       console.log(&quot;EventCtrlsRouter done awaiting a call to Updater.&quot;);
       return rsp;
       break;
    }   
    case &quot;Updater&quot;:
       console.log(&quot;Handling a call from Updater in EventCtrlsRouter.&quot;);
       return 0;
       break;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>We see that depending on the caller, it has an <strong>&quot;EventHandler&quot;</strong> and an <strong>&quot;Updater&quot;</strong> use-case. In the first case it invokes <code>Updater</code>, but in the second it ends its job without calling it. So <code>EventHandler</code>:</p>
<pre><code>async function EventHandler (data) {
  console.log(&quot;EventHandler triggered and awaiting EventCtrlsRouter&quot;);
  let rsp = await EventCtrlsRouter(&quot;EventHandler&quot;,data);
  console.log(&quot;EventHandler done awaiting EventCtrlsRouter&quot;);
  console.log(`Response is ${rsp}`);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Invokes <code>EventCtrlRouter</code> in the <strong>&quot;EventHandler&quot;</strong> use-case, passing the data received. So <code>EventCtrlRouter</code> is in <strong>&quot;EventHandler&quot;</strong> mode, invokes <code>Updater</code> which invokes <code>EventCtrlRouter</code> in <strong>&quot;Updater&quot;</strong> mode. Hance <code>EventCtrlRouter</code> is invoked twice, first by <code>EventHandler</code> and then by <code>Updater</code>, so there will be no endless loops.</p>

Q: Insert new Node inside Node XSLT A: <p>You can match the <code>InformationBody</code> element, copy it as-is, then add an element after it is copied. As below:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;xsl:stylesheet version=&quot;2.0&quot; xmlns:xsl=&quot;http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform&quot;&gt;
    &lt;!-- Identity template, copies everything as is --&gt;
    &lt;xsl:template match=&quot;@*|node()&quot;&gt;
        &lt;xsl:copy&gt;
            &lt;xsl:apply-templates select=&quot;@* | node()&quot;/&gt;
        &lt;/xsl:copy&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
    &lt;!-- Override for target element --&gt;
    &lt;xsl:template match=&quot;InformationBody&quot;&gt;
        &lt;!-- Copy the element --&gt;
        &lt;xsl:copy&gt;
            &lt;!-- And everything inside it --&gt;
            &lt;xsl:apply-templates select=&quot;@* | node()&quot;/&gt;
        &lt;/xsl:copy&gt;
        &lt;!-- Add new node --&gt;
        &lt;xsl:element name=&quot;newNode&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
&lt;/xsl:stylesheet&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Your approach will work the same if there are no elements after <code>InformationBody</code>. If there are, the <code>newNode</code> will be added after all of the children of <code>TAG1</code>.</p>
